    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.077.c1",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "hook",
      "headline": "If you live in France, Brazil or Japan, your law has Roman roots."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.077.c2",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Lawyers who shaped the law",
      "body": "Roman legal experts, the jurists, wrote opinions that developed and explained the law. They held, for example, that the burden of proof rests on the person who makes a claim, not on the one who denies it.",
      "callout": "Burden of proof: whoever claims must prove"
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.077.c3",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Justinian’s code",
      "body": "In the 6th century CE the emperor Justinian had Roman law collected into the Corpus Juris Civilis, the “body of civil law.” It shaped the civil law systems used across continental Europe, Latin America and many other countries."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.077.c4",
      "type": "fact",
      "fact": "English law still speaks Latin.",
      "context": "Many legal terms are Latin words, such as alibi, which means “elsewhere.”"
    },
    {
